    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nCython 3.0–3.3 incorrectly assumes PyErr_Occurred() is non-NULL in async-generator athrow handling. When an injected exception causes the generator to suspend without setting a new exception, this results in PyExceptionClass_Check(NULL) and a segmentation fault.\n\nNative stack (gdb, Cython 3.2.9):\n\n#0  Py_TYPE(ob)                                   ob == NULL\n#1  PyExceptionClass_Check(err)\n#2  __Pyx_PyErr_GivenExceptionMatches2(err=NULL, PyExc_StopAsyncIteration, PyExc_GeneratorExit)\n#3  __Pyx_async_gen_athrow_throw                  (aclose mode, retval != NULL)\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\nclass Suspend:\n    def __await__(self):\n        yield\n\n\nasync def agen():\n    try:\n        yield 1\n    finally:\n        for _ in range(3):\n            try:\n                await Suspend()\n            except ValueError:\n                pass\n\n\ng = agen()\ntry:\n    g.__anext__().send(None)\nexcept StopIteration:\n    pass\ncloser = g.aclose()\ncloser.send(None)           # GeneratorExit goes in, the finally block suspends\ncloser.throw(ValueError())  # the generator suspends again: segfault\n```\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nThe same as CPython: closer.throw(ValueError()) returns None (the value of the next suspension), and the close goes on. Run as plain Python, the code above finishes without error.\n\nActual behaviour: \"Fatal Python error: Segmentation fault\".\n\n### OS\n\nLinux x86_64 (GCC 15). The crash is the same at -O0, -O2 -DNDEBUG and -O3 -DNDEBUG.\n\n### Python version\n\n3.13.2 (also seen in production on 3.13.15)\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.0.12, 3.1.6, 3.2.0, 3.2.9, 3.3.0 and master (418acffa8) all crash.\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
